<!--
  The themed-icon layer (Android 13+). The system tints this with the user's wallpaper colours, so it
  must be a single flat shape: the keyhole is cut out of the shield with evenOdd rather than painted
  over it, because "painted over" means painted in a colour the system is about to replace. None of
  the foreground's bevel or lighting can appear here for the same reason — a one-colour layer has no
  way to express them, and faking them with alpha reads as a smudge under a saturated wallpaper.

  Geometry tracks ic_launcher_foreground exactly. The keyhole is one continuous outline (stem down,
  round the foot, back up, then the long way over the circle) rather than a circle subpath plus an
  overlapping stem — under evenOdd two overlapping subpaths cancel where they cross, which put a
  tinted wedge in the middle of the opening.

  === v2, 2026-07-31 ===============================================================================
  Two changes, both checked against the adaptive mask; the technique above is untouched.

   1. Scaled +7% about (54,54). The silhouette's furthest point moves from 30.3 to 32.4 of the 33
      safe radius, so the mark fills the tile instead of floating in it. Same shoulders, same 0.86
      width-to-height — it is the same shield.
   2. Keyhole enlarged past the uniform scale: radius 6.1 to 7.0, stem half-width 2.3 to 2.6, foot
      dropped to y63. The punch now reads on the 48dp launcher grid and at 19dp in the drawer.
      Still one continuous single-subpath outline, so point 4 above still holds.

  This drawing is also what the app renders in-app: LkLogo's LkMark is this silhouette and keyhole
  flattened onto the 24 grid. Geometry is now centred on (54,54), x 27.9-80.1, y 23.6-84.4.

  === 1a "Slim & sharp", 2026-09-07 ===============================================================
  This file is deliberately untouched by the 1a adoption that re-lit ic_launcher_foreground.xml and
  ic_launcher_background.xml. 1a changed lighting only — diffed against the mark it refines, its
  shield path and keyhole path are character-identical — and a themed layer has no lighting to
  change. Editing it would have moved this silhouette away from the foreground's, which is the one
  thing the paragraph above exists to prevent.
-->
